To draw a basic geometric figure like a triangle with vertices A, B, and C using `tikz`, you would use the following commands:

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
    % Define the coordinates of the vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (B) at (2,3);
    \coordinate (C) at (4,0);

    % Draw the lines connecting the vertices
    \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

    % Label the vertices
    \node at (A) {A};
    \node at (B) {B};
    \node at (C) {C};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```
This code defines the coordinates of the vertices, draws the lines connecting them, and labels the vertices.

To modify the code to draw the triangle with specific styles, such as a dashed line for one side, or to add some text labels along the sides, you can use the following commands:

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
    % Define the coordinates of the vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (B) at (2,3);
    \coordinate (C) at (4,0);

    % Draw the lines connecting the vertices with specific styles
    \draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
    \draw[dashed] (A) -- (C);

    % Label the vertices
    \node at (A) {A};
    \node at (B) {B};
    \node at (C) {C};

    % Add text labels along the sides
    \node at (1,1.5) {Side AB};
    \node at (3,1.5) {Side BC};
    \node at (2,0) {Side AC};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```
This code adds a dashed line for the side AC and includes text labels along the sides of the triangle.

A specific 2x2 rotation matrix in $U(2)$ is:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(\theta) & -\sin(\theta) \\
\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}
\]
To verify that it is unitary, we check that its conjugate transpose is equal to its inverse:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) \\
-\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(\theta) & -\sin(\theta) \\
\sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta)
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\]
This confirms that the matrix is unitary. Thank you for the help.

You would need to use the \textcolor{} command from the xcolor package to change the text color. Here's how you can modify the \highlighttext command:

```latex
\usepackage{xcolor}
\newcommand{\highlighttext}[1]{\textcolor{red}{\textbf{\textit{#1}}}}
```

This command will now make the text bold, italicized, and red.

You can make the \highlighttext command more flexible by adding optional arguments for bold and italic styles. Here's how you can modify the command:

```latex
\usepackage{xcolor}
\newcommand{\highlighttext}[3][red]{\textcolor{#1}{\ifnum#2=1 \textbf{#3}\fi\ifnum#3=1 \textit{#3}\fi}}
```

In this command, the first argument is optional and defaults to red. The second and third arguments are required and determine whether the text should be bold and italicized, respectively. The \ifnum command is used to check the values of the second and third arguments and apply the corresponding styles.

This allows the user to customize the appearance of the text by specifying the desired styles. For example:

```latex
\highlighttext[blue]{1}{1}{This text will be blue, bold, and italicized.}
\highlighttext[green]{1}{0}{This text will be green and bold.}
\highlighttext[purple]{0}{1}{This text will be purple and italicized.}
```

This approach provides more flexibility and control over the formatting of the text.

